# Normalise line endings in the repository so that a Windows checkout and a
# Linux CI runner produce byte-identical files. Without this, `core.autocrlf=true`
# on Windows rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout, and `gofmt -l` (which requires LF)
# then flags every Go file locally while the same commit is clean in CI.
#
# Windows contributors: ALSO run this once, in the repo —
#
#     git config core.autocrlf false
#
# `.gitattributes` governs what is stored and what CI sees (always LF), but a
# global `core.autocrlf=true` can still materialise CRLF in your working tree on
# some Git for Windows versions, which makes `make check` fail locally for no
# real reason. Setting it locally removes the ambiguity.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must keep CRLF to remain double-clickable.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# Binary assets — never touched by EOL normalisation.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webmanifest binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.exe   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary

# Keep generated/vendored trees out of language tooling and diffs.
web/node_modules/   binary
web/.nuxt/          binary
web/.output/        binary
web/_out_old/       binary
